        narration: 'Among the tall silver reeds beside Moonbeam Lake lived a tiny young firefly named Lily. Every twilight, when the purple shadows stretched gently across the water and crickets tuned their soft evening fiddles, Lily’s older cousins soared into the night air glowing like hundreds of drifting golden lanterns. Look at them swoop in sparkling figure-eights above the water lilies! Look how bright their lanterns shone—almost like fallen stars dancing on velvet! Lily, however, felt very small and very dim. Her tiny tail lantern gave only a soft, pale lemon flicker that sometimes blinked right out when she grew nervous. While the graceful dragonflies shimmered like blue jewels by day and the nightingales sang enchanting songs by night, Lily often hid inside a curled fern leaf, whispering wistfully, "I cannot sing like the birds, I cannot shimmer in sunlight like the butterflies, and my little glow is barely bright enough to light up a single dewdrop."',
